findings.why:
`f"Module: {path}

{untrusted(...)}"` places the module path outside the data block. Paths come from git and can contain attacker-controlled characters (arbitrary bytes, newlines via `git` path quoting aside, paths are valid UTF-8 with odd characters like newlines are not legal in git tree entries... actually git forbids '\0' but allows '
'). A repo with a directory containing `</untrusted>` or prompt-injection text in the path would inject instructions outside the data block, breaking the 'repository content is data' guarantee. The path is also used inside the untrusted source attribute (via f-string interpolation into the HTML attribute) without escaping quotes, allowing breaking out of the attribute.
